#import "../../template.typ": *

#plant-header("Dark Knight Bluebeard", "Caryopteris x clandonensis 'Dark Knight'", "bluebeard")[
  Dark Knight Bluebeard produces vivid deep-blue flower spikes in late July–September, a season when little else is blooming. Extremely fragrant, attracting butterflies, bees, and hummingbirds heavily. Low, mounded habit (2–3 ft. tall and wide). Cut to the ground each spring — it blooms on new wood. DRAINAGE WARNING: This is the most drainage-sensitive shrub in your landscape. On drip irrigation.
]

=== Watering

On drip irrigation with a *0.5 gph emitter*. See the Irrigation & Watering section for the shared drip schedule. Dark Knight Bluebeard actively prefers dry-to-average conditions. In clay soil, overwatering is a genuine kill risk — a single waterlogging event in late fall or early winter can cause root rot. The schedule's natural taper in September–October aligns well with this plant's needs; stop drip support by late September.

=== Fertilization

Apply a very light N-K formula in early spring as new growth emerges — avoid any phosphorus-containing product. A single light feeding is sufficient. Avoid excess nitrogen, which causes rank leafy growth and reduces the flower display.

=== Pruning & Maintenance

Cut the entire plant back to 4–6 inches from the ground in early spring (late March–April) as buds begin to swell. This is mandatory — Bluebeard dies back significantly in Zone 5b winters. Do not be alarmed if there is no visible life until May; it emerges late. Do not cut back in fall — the stems provide some crown insulation through the winter.

=== Wildlife Value

Dark Knight Bluebeard is among the best late-season pollinator plants in your landscape. The deep blue flowers from July through September attract an extraordinary variety of butterflies — monarchs, painted ladies, swallowtails, and sulphurs in particular — along with hummingbirds and bumble bees at a time when most other flowers have finished. This is one of the most important late-season food sources for monarchs on their southward migration through Colorado. House finches occasionally visit spent flower heads for seed. Cottontail rabbits may nibble young spring growth before the plant has fully leafed out.

=== Pests & Disease

Bluebeard has few serious pest or disease problems when drainage is adequate. In wet conditions, #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=caryopteris+root+rot+crown+rot+treatment")[crown rot] is the primary issue — there is no treatment once root rot takes hold. The remedy is entirely preventive: excellent drainage, raised planting, and reduced fall/winter watering. Occasional #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=spider+mites+bluebeard+treatment")[spider mites] in hot dry periods — treat with horticultural oil. In Colorado's dry climate, fungal foliar diseases are uncommon.

=== Other Care

CLAY SOIL IS THE PRIMARY RISK. Caryopteris requires excellent drainage year-round and will reliably rot in wet clay over winter. To grow this successfully: plant in a raised bed or significantly amended mound (mix native soil with 40–50% coarse grit and compost), plant so the crown sits 2–3 inches above surrounding grade. A south or west-facing slope is ideal. Without these amendments, winter survival in your clay soil is unlikely. Container growing is a reliable alternative.
